ze this?\" [564] He waved the object in his hand, which Virginia did not find particularly interesting. [565] \"Mrs. Ruef, can you tell me what this is?\" [566] \"It seems like a letter,\" Virginia replied, feeling that the person in front of her was somewhat eccentric. [567] \"Then look at who the letter is addressed to,\" he said, handing her the letter with a meaningful look. [568] \"I can read,\" Virginia said pleasantly, \"It's addressed to Captain O'Neil at 15 Paris Fish Circle.\" [569] He seemed to search her face for something, but found nothing. [570] \"Are you going to read the letter?\" [571] Virginia took the envelope from him and pulled out the letter, scanning it quickly before handing it back. [572] \"It's a private letter, I shouldn't look at it.\" [573] The man chuckled sarcastically. [574] \"Mrs. Rue, your performance was truly remarkable, commendable. But you can't deny your signature, can you?\" [575] \"Signature?\" [576] Virginia flipped the letter over, her eyes widening in surprise. The letter was written in elegant cursive, bearing the signature \"Virginia Ruff.\" She refrained from exclaiming, carefully reading the letter again. She stood there, pondering for a moment. The letter made it clear what was about to happen. [577] \"Is that enough, ma'am?\" the man asked. \"Is that your name?\" [578] \"Yes,\" Virginia said, \"it is my name.\" She swallowed the rest of the sentence, \"but it's not my handwriting,\" and smiled at her visitor. [579] \"Well then,\" she said gently, \"let's sit down and chat.\" [580] He was taken aback by her response. He could sense that she was not afraid. [581] \"First, I'd like to know how you found me.\" [582] \"It's quite simple,\" he replied. [583] He reached into his pocket and handed her a piece of paper torn from a newspaper. Anthony Kede would have recognized it immediately. [584] Virginia handed the paper back to him, frowning slightly and thinking. [585] \"I see,\" she said. \"It's indeed quite easy.\" [586] \"Mrs. Rue, you must know that there are other letters besides this one.\" [587] \"Oh, I'm so careless,\" Virginia said. [588] She knew that this light tone would only make him more confused, and she found it amusing. [589] \"Nevertheless,\" she smiled at him kindly, \"thank goodness you came to return these letters to me.\" [590] He cleared his throat and paused, \"I am a poor man, Mrs. Rue.\" He looked serious. [591] \"People say that you will go to heaven,\" she said. [592] \"I can't give you these letters without some compensation.\" [593] \"You may be mistaken, these letters are the property of the writer.\" [594] \"Madam, that is the law. But in this country, you often say 'possession is nine-tenths of the law.' And are you going to sue me?\" [595] \"Extortion is a serious crime in the law,\" Virginia reminded him. [596] \"Come on, Mrs. Rue, I'm not stupid. I've read these letters, they are love letters from a woman to her lover, full of fear of being discovered by her husband. Are you asking me to give them to your husband?\" [597] \"You've overlooked one possibility. What if my husband has already passed away?\" [598] He confidently shook his head. [599] \"Then you have nothing to fear and wouldn't be sitting here negotiating with me.\" [600] Virginia smiled. [601] \"How much do you want?\" she asked, taking a practical tone. [602] \"Give me a thousand pounds, and I'll give you all the letters back. I'm asking for very little, I don't like this business.\" [603] \"I wouldn't pay you a thousand pounds even in my dreams,\" Virginia said firmly. [604] \"Madam, I don't haggle. A thousand pounds, and I'll give you all the letters back.\" [605] Virginia thought for a moment. [606] \"Give me some time to think it over. It's not easy to come up with such a large sum of money.\" [607] \"Well, pay me fifty pounds now, and I'll come back later,\" Virginia looked at her watch, it was 4:05, she seemed to have heard the doorbell. [608] \"Alright,\" she said hurriedly, \"come back tomorrow, but later, around six o'clock.\" [609] She walked to the desk by the wall and opened a drawer, taking out a disorganized stack of bills. [610] \"Here's about forty pounds, is that enough?\" [611] He grabbed the money from her hand. [612] \"Now, please leave immediately.\" Virginia said. [613] He obediently left the room. Through the crack of the door, Virginia saw George Romax standing in the hallway, and Chifford leading him up the stairs. The door closed, and Virginia called out loudly, \"Come over here and bring the tea, will you?\" [614] She opened both windows in the room, and George Romax entered, finding her standing there upright and alert, with her hair blown by the wind. [615] \"I'll close the windows in a moment, I just wanted to let some fresh air in. Did you run into the extortionist in the hallway?\" [616] \"What extortioner?\" [617] \"Extortioner, George, ex-ter-min-a-tor, someone who extorts money.\" [618] \"Oh, Virginia, you're joking, right?\" [619] \"I'm serious, George.\" [620] \"But who is he extorting?\" [621] \"Me.\" [622] \"What did you do?\" [623] \"Coincidentally, this time I really didn't do anything. The gentleman mistook me for someone else.\" [624] \"Did you report it?\" [625] \"No, do you think I should?\" [626] \"Well,\" George thoughtfully considered, \"no, no, maybe not. Your approach is wise. This could involve you and have people talking. Perhaps, you would even have to testify in court.\" [627] \"If that's the case, I'd quite enjoy it,\" Virginia said. \"I would love to be summoned, to see if those judges really do tell old, stale jokes. It would be too amusing. A few days ago, I went to Vine Street to report a missing diamond brooch, and I met a very charming inspector there, the nicest person I've ever seen.\" [628] George was used to her trivial matters and didn't pay much attention. [629] \"So, what are you going to do about this scoundrel?\" [630] \"I just let him do it.\" [631] \"How?\" [632] \"Blackmail me.\" [633] George's face contorted in extreme terror, and Virginia unconsciously bit her lower lip. [634] \"You mean, you didn't clarify it with him?\" [635] Virginia shook her head and glanced at him. [636] \"Oh, Virginia, you're crazy!\" [637] \"I knew you'd say that.\" [638] \"But why? Why did you do it?\" [639] \"For many reasons. First, his blackmailing technique was exquisite, and I didn't want to ruin a masterpiece of an artist; and, you know, I've never been blackmailed before.\" [640] \"I'm glad to hear that.\" [641] \"I wanted to see what it felt like.\" [642] \"I can't understand it, Virginia.\" [643] \"I know you don't understand.\" [644] \"Did you give him any money?\" [645] \"A little.\" Virginia apologized. [646] \"How much?\" [647] \"Forty pounds.\" [648] \"Virginia!\" [649] \"Dear George, it's just the price of one of my evening gowns. New experiences and new clothes are equally thrilling, actually, even more so.\" [650] George could only shake his head. At that moment, Chifley entered with the teapot. George suppressed his anger and did not let it out. [651] Virginia skillfully picked up the heavy silver tea pot and poured the tea, continuing the previous conversation. [652] \"I have an even smarter and better idea. It's said that women are like cats, but today I helped another woman a big favor, and now this man will not look for another Virginia Rue. He thought he had found his target. The poor woman must have been trembling when she wrote the letter, and it would have been easy for him to blackmail her. But it's not so easy now, and he doesn't even know it. As the book says, I am guiltless, and he is at my mercy. George, this is the art of game and counter-game.\" [653] George shook his head after hearing her words. [654] \"I don't want you to do this,\" he insisted. \"I don't want you to do this.\" [655] \"It's okay, George. By the way, you didn't come to discuss blackmail. You came to see me, didn't you? The correct answer is 'to see you.' Accent on 'you,' and then firmly take her hand. But if you just ate a cream scone, don't use your hands, just use your eyes.\" [656] \"I came to see you,\" George answered seriously. \"I'm glad to see you alone at home.\" [657] \"Ah, George, I'm defenseless.\" [658] \"I want to ask you for a favor, Virginia. In my heart, you've always been a charming person.\" [659] \"You!\" [660] \"And wise.\" [661] \"Really? A man understands me!\" [662] \"There's a young man coming to England tomorrow, and I want you to meet him.\" [663] \"Sure, George, but it's your territory.\" [664] \"You'll be charming if you want to.\" [665] Virginia tilted her head slightly. [666] \"George, you know, my 'charm' isn't something I do on purpose. I like people, and they like me. But to coldly go and seduce a stranger, I can't do that. There are women who do that better than me.\" [667] \"I wouldn't ask you to do that. This young man is Canadian, his name is McGregor...\"\n[668] \"A Canadian with Scottish blood.\" She cleverly deduced. [669] \"He may not have interacted with British upper-class people, so I want him to see the true charm and elegance of a British lady.\" [670] \"You mean me?\" [671] \"Of course.\" [672] \"Why?\" [673] \"What do you mean?\" [674] \"I mean, why? Do you really promote the image of a genuine British lady to every Canadian visitor? George, what's the point of this? To put it crudely, what do you want?\" [675] \"I don't understand why you care about this, Virginia.\" [676] \"Why would I waste an evening of my time doing that without knowing the reason behind it?\" [677] \"Your way of understanding things is quite unique, everyone would think\u2026\"\n[678] \"Is that so? Come on, George, tell me.\" [679] \"There's been some tension in a country in Central Europe recently. The reason doesn't matter, what's important is that Mr. McGregor needs to understand that the monarchist restoration in Hesse is crucial for the peace of the whole of Europe.\" [680] \"European peace is just a bunch of nonsense,\" Virginia said calmly, \"but monarchy, I'm all for it, especially for a unique nation like Hesse. Are you proposing a Hesse king, then? Who is it?\" [681] George didn't want to answer this question, but he didn't know how to avoid it. This conversation was completely different from what he had expected. He had thought that Virginia would be a docile tool, willingly accepting his instructions and not asking any embarrassing questions. But it was the opposite. She seemed determined to pry to the bottom of things, and George didn't believe in women's caution, not wanting her to know anything. He had made a mistake; Virginia was not the kind of woman he thought she was. In fact, she might cause big trouble. What she had said about her negotiations with the extortionist made him deeply worried. Virginia was not reliable at all; she had no idea how to handle serious matters. [682] \"Prince Michael O'Paulwich.\" [683] \"George, stop playing around. The newspapers are already full of rumors, either praising the O'Paulwich dynasty or exaggerating the martyrdom of the late Nicolas IV. He's no longer the foolish husband of a third-rate actress, but has become the embodiment of saints and heroes.\" [684] George recoiled, becoming more and more convinced that he had made a grave mistake in seeking Virginia's help. He had to stop himself from going further. [685] \"You're right, Virginia,\" he said hastily, getting up to leave, \"I shouldn't have asked you to do that. But we're in a hurry to let the government know our views on the Hesslar crisis and reach a consensus with us. And I think Mr. McGloughlin has some influence in the media. I know you're a passionate monarchist and have a deep understanding of this country, so that's why I asked you to help.\" [686] \"Is that your explanation?\" [687] \"Yes, but I don't think you're interested.\" [688] Virginia looked at him for a second and then laughed. [689] \"George,\" she said, \"your lies are terrible.\" [690] \"Virginia!\" [691] \"Terrible, absolutely terrible. If I were you, I'd come up with a better lie, at least something that could be believed. Little George, don't worry, I'll figure out what's going on myself. By the weekend, at the Chimney House, I'll know more about the McGrath mystery, I suppose.\" [692] \"The Chimney House? Are you going to the Chimney House?\" [693] George was flustered, hoping to find the Duke of Cambridge quickly and prevent him from inviting Virginia. [694] \"Bundel called me this morning.\" [695] George didn't give up his last attempt. [696] \"The party will be boring,\" he said. \"You won't like it.\" [697] \"George, can't you trust me and tell me the truth? It's not too late.\" [698] George took her hand, then gently let it go. [699] \"I've already told you the truth,\" he said coldly. [700] \"This lie is much better than the last one,\" Virginia said approvingly. \"But it's not great. Cheer up, I'll go to the Chimney House, and I'll play my charms, just like you said. Life suddenly became very interesting. First, a blackmailer, then a George troubled by diplomatic affairs. The poor woman begs him to believe her and be honest with her. Will he accept? No, he's determined to keep it a secret. Goodbye, George. Look at me one last time before you go. Alright, dear, don't be upset.\" [701] As soon as George walked out the door, Virginia rushed to the phone. [702] She dialed the number she wanted and found Miss Elinor Brent. [703] \"Is it you, Bunder? I'll be at the Chimney House tomorrow. What? Boring? No, it won't be. Bunder, nothing can stop me! That's it!\" [704] Chapter Seven: Mr. McGlass refused the invitation\n[705] The letters disappeared. [706] The letters had indeed vanished, and Anthony had to accept the fact. He was well aware that he could not chase after Giuseppe in the corridors of the Bretton Hotel, as it would not only be futile but also attract unnecessary attention. [707] He assumed that Giuseppe mistakenly thought the package was a memoir. When he realized the mistake, he would likely try to retrieve the memoir again. Anthony had to be prepared. [708] Anthony suddenly thought of a cautious and feasible way to advertise to find those letters. If Giuseppe was sent by the \"Red Hand League\" or hired by the Royalists - the latter possibility seemed more likely, these letters would be of no use to either side, so Giuseppe should be willing to return them and make some money. Anthony thought it through and then went back to bed, hoping Giuseppe would not be so desperate to come again that night. [709] When Anthony woke up, he had a well-thought-out plan. He had a hearty breakfast and flipped through the newspaper, which was full of news about the discovery of oil fields in Hessoslovakia. He then requested a meeting with the hotel manager. [710] The hotel manager was a refined Frenchman who received Anthony in his office. [711] \"You want to see me, Mr. McGlass?\u201d\n[712] \"Yes. I checked in here yesterday afternoon and had dinner in my room, and one of the waiters, Gisippus, seemed a bit strange to me.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."